 difficult for us  and as you say learn to live with the  virus  that does mean that we're going to have  to treat it  carefully we're going to have to keep on  monitoring the cases  and we're going to have to ensure that  every adult gets double vaxxed  because that is the key to keeping the  virus under control as we move into the  autumn  and the winter that's admirably clear  and  it looks as though you are following the  prime minister's  suggestion that um we will be put  lifting the restrictions but the bma the  british medical  association is urging caution for  example keeping the requirement for face  masks in public places  now um mr general will you be dumping  your mask on july the 20th  well like many people i want to get away  from these restrictions as quickly as i  possibly can and we don't want them to  stay in place for a day longer  than is necessary we are going to i  think now move into a period  where there won't be legal restrictions  the state won't be telling you what to  do but you will want to exercise  a degree of personal responsibility and  judgment so different people will come  to different conclusions on things like  masks for example and the prime minister  will set out more detail on  the the national policy on some of those  restrictions in  the coming days but there will be things  that we all  definitely need to do as i say it will  be essential  that every adult gets fully vaccinated  and there are still people  in the most significant categories  categories 1 to 9  who haven't been vaccinated 50 000  people in those categories got  vaccinated  in the last week they've come forward  relatively late in the process but  it's really important that we get those  remaining people  vaccinated it will also be important  that we do things like  making sure that the most vulnerable get  their flu vaccinations  this autumn and winter because that  would be a big issue there will be  things we need to do as a society  to make sure that we keep through we get  through this but before we  before we go on to that can we just deal  with a question i asked you  if you are permitted will you  be getting rid of your mask i i will i i  don't  particularly want to wear a mask i don't  think  a lot of people uh enjoy doing it  we will be moving into a phase though  where these will be matters of personal  choice  and so some member society will want to  do so  for perfectly legitimate reasons but it  will be a different  period where we as private citizens  make these judgments rather than the  government telling you what to do  okay now apparently today is national  thank you day whose idea was that and  um what would you the government like  nation to be doing once they stopped  watching this program  well it was actually a bottom-up idea it  came from local communities  uh they came together and established  this idea that  this is a moment in which we can all go  and say a big thank you to those who've  helped us  in our daily lives over the course of  the last year perhaps those people  who've gone out of their  way to support the community through  covert 19 or others  who've done good work the prime minister  will be hosting  some of those who've helped us through  the pandemic at number 10  and i know people in my own community  that i would like to say  a big thank you to isn't this a bit  previous  you've just advised me to wait for the  prime minister's  statement on july the 19th but you're  encouraging everybody to  barbecue today i mean couldn't this have  waited a couple of weeks until  we actually know what the guidance is  going to be  isn't this yet another case of the  government sending mixed messages  no the government didn't create uh the  actual thank you day it's actually been  created by members  you didn't have to back it today we can  say thank you anytime in the next  six months if we want to well to say the  government didn't create  this day it's been created by mems the  public who themselves wanted to say  thank you of course uh there are  guidelines in place that you have to  follow if you're  holding an event today and i'm sure the  prime minister and others who  who are doing so will make sure that  they are followed but the point of the  day  uh is for those of us who benefited from  the hard work and compassion empathy  that others have provided over the  course of last year  to reach out to them and say thank you  and that can be done in many ways  there may be some people who are holding  socially distanced get-togethers  but there'll be plenty of other people  who might use this opportunity simply to  pick up the phone to somebody  who's done good work and really help  them out over the course of the last  year  yes indeed we can say thank you in many  ways wouldn't one way  be for the health secretary or the prime  minister  to pick up the phone to the let's say  the royal college of nursing or the bma  and say you know what we decided that  one percent  pay rise which is below inflation is  going to be boosted  by two or three percent that would be a  real way to say thank you wouldn't it  well we all want to reward those people  who've worked in the nhs and social  carers local government secretary  i feel very strongly about that that at  the moment is a topic for the  independent pay review body  who will be providing advice to the  health secretary  and the government in the coming months  and armed with that  we'll make a decision but it's only in  my role i've always  driven to provide local government with  the resource it needs  to fund social care we've done that over  the course of the pandemic we've now  done it  with two record-breaking settlements for  the local government sector since boris  johnson became  prime minister and i very much hope will  be in a position to do that in the  future  so uh we're not stuck to the one percent  it could be higher in the end  well the process is that uh the  recommendation  is judged by the independent pay review  body and then it comes to ministers for  a final  decision so nothing is is set in stone  but we as ministers will have to wait  for the advice that we receive from the  pay review body and that's the right  thing to do  right well let's keep talking about how  we say thank you you say in your release  that you want to thank people who have  provided refuge with women  facing domestic abuse which everybody  agrees  has risen during lockdown so  now women who are afraid to venture out  onto the high street  in case they run into their abusers are  being urged  to rock up today to a cheery barbecue  whilst having nowhere to go  later because of lack of funds for  refugees now that would be a good way of  saying thank you and that's i think  that's in your bailiwick isn't it  why not say thank you today with some  more money  for domestic abuse refuges for the women  who have suffered violence  well trevor we are doing that we passed  the domestic abuse act  earlier in the year which is a landmark  piece of legislation  to ensure that women and men who are  victims of domestic abuse  get the support that they need in safe  and secure accommodation  such as refuges we've fully funded that  major commitment  with 125 million pounds and that money  is now with local councils  who are ensuring that local services get  the support that they need and in fact i  spoke  last week at the women's aid conference  to the brilliant people who run those  refuges  across the country to say thank you to  them and also  wrote to every local council in the  country urging them  to use that money wisely and amongst  other services that they commission  to make sure that they properly fund  women's refuges  because they do a fantastic job they  have often been funded in quite a  hand-to-mouth way  in recent decades that needs to come to  an end now that we've got this very  significant amount  of extra funding available for them did  the leaders of women's aid read out to  you what they said in their report a  couple of months ago  which is uh the 90 million announced in  the budget  today is not enough they then go on to  say  that they need 393 million  pounds for of funding and that there is  a shortfall of 200 million pounds  in the government's current plans  what are you going to do about that well  i i don't recognize those numbers  they're not ones i've actually seen  the the amount of funding the amount of  funding i'm reading it out to you  i understand that but the amount of  money that we've provided 125 million  pounds  is actually considered to be fully  funding  the refuge and domestic abuse  uh duties that now fall on local houses  but not by women's  aid well i had a i had very good  conversation with  the chief executive women's aide and  with representatives  of those who run refuges during the week  they appreciate the funding of course  people would always like to have  more money but this is a very  significant increase in the amount of  funding  that any government has given in the  past for women's refugees and for other  services they'll be provided  for victims of domestic abuse the key  thing now  is to make sure that councils perform  their duties  under this new piece of legislation  correctly and they include  giving women and other victims of  domestic abuse priority  for social housing if they need to leave  their home  ensuring that they get the support  services that they need that can be  delivered in many different settings and  as i say making sure that refuges  across the country are put on a  sustainable financial footing  so we don't see the situation that i'm  afraid we have done in the past  which is where some of those refugees  are having to kind of survive  year in year out with too little money  and not  long-term sustainable funding we're  going to change that  well perhaps you need to have another  conversation with them but let's let's  talk about um  battley and spent um you lost when you  might have won do you blame matt hancock  well of course i'm disappointed that we  lost we had a great candidate ryan  stevens and i'd love to have seen him  in parliament but governments very very  rarely take  seats off oppositions in by-elections in  fact there's only been one  instance in recent years which was  hartley paul which was an exceptional  result actually this was the biggest  swing  from an opposition to the party of  government  for 39 years so in my lifetime  so it was still a fantastic result for  the conservative party albeit not  exactly what we would have hoped for um  and i think there are a lot of questions  for kierstarma as to how the labour  party  are just scraping home by 300 or so  votes  when they should be romping home in  these sorts of elections  okay well um here's a question for you  um  given that you had a 8 000 vote leg up  from  george galloway who took votes from  labour  actually it wasn't that good a result  for you and surely there's something  more fundamental going on here as there  was in cheshire  and amisham could it be that like in  cheshire many people in those  very pretty villages uh around batley  think that your planning reforms mean  that  your idea of leveling up is allowing  developers to concrete  over their villages and fields  no i don't agree with that at all i mean  on the question of  the by-elections as i say governments  extremely rarely take seats off  the opposition in by-elections of course  it was also disappointing result in  cheshire and amisham  but the argument put out there very  disingenuously  by the lib dems that there were huge  amounts of houses being built in that  area  uh was simply untrue last year only a  hundred houses  were built in the cheshire amisham  constituency which i think was the  tenth lowest of any local authority in  the whole country  what we want to see are good quality  environmentally sustainable homes with  the infrastructure around them  being built across the country so that  young people and families can get onto  the housing ladder  that's absolutely critical to levelling  up to  tackling intergenerational unfairnesses  in this country  and we're going to redouble our efforts  to build those homes but do so in a way  that protects green spaces  they won't be being built on the green  belt and we want to make a planning  system  which is much more engaging than the  current one in which only one or two  percent  of the public have any contact with in  their lifetimes  when we're talking about um leveling up  on friday you were in a place called  kidsgrove which is near stoke on trent  you were talking about the importance of  the investments that you're making  locally and you tweeted leveling up in  action  where 20 17 million pounds of investment  from the towns fund  has enabled the reopening of the sport  center  there in kidsgrove um would it not have  been  simpler if the conservative-led  staffordshire county council hadn't  withdrawn their substitute subsidy in  2017  to that sports center in the first place  then you wouldn't have had to make that  investment  well the towns fund is part of our now  almost 10 billion pound investment into  towns and smaller cities across the  country  aimed to improve living standards  improve quality of life  and i was really pleased to go to to  kidsgrove by stoke on trent  as you say on friday and see how it's  impacting people's lives  there the decision actually was taken by  a labour council  there it was very unfortunate as a  result of the town's fund we're changing  that  and that sports centre is being brought  back into community use  as we speak and i'm hoping that we'll be  able to do more things like that across  the country  particularly examples like that where  it's actually community  ownership that's a sports centre that's  going to be run by the local community  itself not by a council  but by a group of engaged local  residents coming together and we're  putting  funding available for those sorts of  schemes to happen right across the  country  one last very quick question if i may uh  mr generic  um last week was the fourth anniversary  of the awful events  at the grenfell tower and the government  has been making  quite a lot of money available to repair  cladding but the estimates uh  to deal with the range of  buildings across the country um  are as high as 15 billion pounds and  you've made about three and a half  billion pounds available  that means that many uh flat owners  across the country are having to pay and  we've seen  lots of um reports this week are having  to pay  the price of their entire properties  in order to repair the cladding that  surely cannot be right  well i have huge sympathy with those uh  leaseholders who are placed in an  impossible situation  the government is actually putting over  5 billion pounds  towards this issue we're ensuring that  the most dangerous material  acm cladding and other unsafe forms of  cladding are removed from all high-rise  buildings in the country  at no cost to leaseholders that work is  underway now we're making  good progress we're also  trying to ensure there's a much more  sensible and proportionate approach  to risk on other buildings particularly  the lower rise ones  because we are seeing examples of  leaseholders being asked to pay bills  for works which seem to be unnecessary  there's no actually higher risk of an  individual  living in a a purpose-built flat or a  high-rise building than in any other  building  in fact more people die in fires in  houses and bungalows  than they do in high-rise apartments so  we're working with lenders and insurers  and surveyors  to try to create a much more sensible  and proportionate way forward  mr generic thank you very much indeed

